{
  "transcript": "Push, pull. This really is the core idea of flirtation, of teasing. You have a positive and a negative. Think of the old cartoon where the girl pulls the petals off a flower. He loves me. He loves me not. He loves me. He loves me not. That tension, she doesn't a 100% know whether you've actually bought into her or not. That's the whole thing. Because there's both a positive and a negative spike, it stays engaging. It stays emotionally relevant, and it makes her want to win you over, want to impress you. The simplest version is just, I love you, but I hate you. Part of me loves you. Part of me kind of hates you. Or I thought I loved you until you said that. You were so cool until you just blew it. But here's what most guys miss. You can't just say it flat. You have to leave some tension. It's exactly like a comedian. He sets up one expectation then changes it on you, and that sudden surprise is what makes it funny. With a push pull, that's what makes it work. Say it with no inflection, no setup, no pause, and it dies. And both halves are doing real work. A straight compliment on its own makes you needy. You've shown too much interest too early too easily. Add the negative and you're pushing her away a little. And when you push someone away, it's a natural instinct for her to try and prove herself to win you back. You become the thing she has to earn instead of the thing she already has. By metering out the good stuff, by making her actually work for it, when she finally does win you over, it feels better. She values it more. The same move also sets the premise. For anything to turn romantic, she has to feel that you see her that way. Most guys do that by showing interest, which is exactly when they get needy. You can do it in the negative instead. Instead of, I think you're really cute. You say, don't think you can get away with that just because you're cute. You've acknowledged it. This is man to woman. It's on the table. But as a fact, not a favor. You're not won over yet and she's going to have to do more. And this goes well beyond dating. That same instinct, warm but with a little edge is what makes people take you seriously anywhere. We trust the praise of someone who isn't easy to impress. So here's the move. A few minutes a day, say the first thing you notice then flip it. A positive falls to a negative. A negative falls to a positive. Keep at it and push pull stops being something you reach for and becomes second nature. The line just shows up right when you need it. So put in the reps. That's how you stop being the easy yes and become the one she's trying to win over.",
